Ladybird hosts an extension’s background page in a hidden headless
view in its own WebContent process, loading the manifest’s page or a
generated page of script tags, and gives any document at the
extension’s own URL the browser namespace when it becomes active.
WebKit’s WebExtensionContext::loadBackgroundContent() creates a
hidden WKWebView for backgroundContentURL(), which is the manifest’s
page or the generatedBackgroundContent() page of script tags, and
WebExtensionControllerProxy::globalObjectIsAvailableForFrame()
installs the namespace on a main-world frame whose URL belongs to the
extension (extensionContext(frame.url())). Blink’s MV2 background
page is a BackgroundContents hosted by ExtensionHost
(extensions/browser/extension_host.cc) at _generated_background_page
.html from GenerateBackgroundPageContents(), and its MV3 background
is a service worker started by ServiceWorkerTaskQueue; Dispatcher::
DidCreateScriptContext() registers any chrome-extension:// frame as a
privileged context. Gecko’s BackgroundPage (ext-backgroundPage.js) is
a HiddenExtensionPage loading _generated_background_page.html, and
ExtensionPolicyService::CheckDocument() initializes an extension
document through ProcessScript().InitExtensionDocument() when its
principal carries an add-on policy. Tearing the host down before the
rest of the Application is Ladybird-specific plumbing.
